\s57 \li1512 \fi-806 \ri1007 \ql \tx1461 \f0 \b \fs52 \f5 \b0 \fs73 p\tab \f0 \b \fs52 {{\NeXTHelpMarker423 \markername NS_DEV_DOCFOR:objc_protocol:NSTableDataSource;}
¬}\pard \s57 \li1512 \fi-806 \ri1007 \ql \tx1461 NSTableDataSource\
\pard \s30 \li1520 \fi-8 \ri1007 \ql \fs28 (informal protocol)\
\fs20\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s8 \li4233 \fi-2721 \ri1007 \ql \tx4233 \f0 \b \fs24 Category Of:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 NSObject\
\fs20\
\s22 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 Declared In:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 AppKit/NSTableView.h\
\fs20\
\fs50\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s7 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Category Description\
\fs14\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \f1 \b0 \fs28 The NSTableDataSource category declares the methods that an NSTableView uses to access the contents of its data source object. It determines how many rows to display by sending a \b numberOfRowsInTableView:\b0  message, and accesses individual values with the \b tableView:objectValueForTableColumn:row:\b0  and \b tableView:setObjectValue:forTableColumn:row:\b0  methods. A data source must implement the first two methods to work with an NSTableView, but if it doesn\'27t implement the third the NSTableView simply provides read-only access to its contents.\
\fs14\
\fs28 The NSTableView treats objects provided by its data source as values to be displayed in NSCell objects. If these objects aren\'27t of common value classes\'d0such as NSString, NSNumber, and so on\'d0you\'27ll need to create a custom NSFormatter to display them. See the NSFormatter class specification for more information.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Suppose that an NSTableView\'27s column identifiers are set up as NSStrings containing the names of attributes for the column, such as \'aaLast Name\'ba, \'aaCity\'ba, and so on, and that the data source stores its records as an NSMutableArray, called \b records\b0 , of NSMutableDictionary objects using those names as keys. Here\'27s a small example, given as an ASCII property list:\
\fs14\
\pard \s20 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2413 \tx2889 \tx3421 \tx3953 \tx4457 \tx4933 \tx7005 \tx7509 \tx8013 \tx8517 \tx9021 \tx9525 \tx10029 \tx10533 \tx11037 \tx11513 \f2 \fs24 (\
\fi0   \{ "Last Name" = Anderson;\
\fi0     "First Name" = James;\
\fi0     Abode = apartment;\
\fi0     City = "San Francisco";\
\fi0   \},\
\fi0   \{ "Last Name" = Beresford;\
\fi0     "First Name" = Keith;\
\fi0     Abode = apartment;\
\fi0     City = "Redwood City";\
\fi0   \}\
\fi0 )\
\fs14\
\f1 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 With such a record structure, this implementation of \b tableView:objectValueForTableColumn:row:\b0  suffices to retrieve values for the NSTableView:\
\fs14\
\pard \s20 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2413 \tx2889 \tx3421 \tx3953 \tx4457 \tx4933 \tx7005 \tx7509 \tx8013 \tx8517 \tx9021 \tx9525 \tx10029 \tx10533 \tx11037 \tx11513 \f2 \fs24 - (id)tableView:(NSTableView\'80*)aTableView\
\fi0     objectValueForTableColumn:(NSTableColumn\'80*)aTableColumn\
\fi0     row:(int)rowIndex\
\fi0 \{\
\fi0     id theRecord, theValue;\
\fi0 \
    NSParameterAssert(rowIndex >= 0 && rowIndex < [records count]);\
\fi0     theRecord = [records objectAtIndex:rowIndex];\
\fi0     theValue = [theRecord objectForKey:[aTableColumn identifier]];\
\fi0     return theValue;\
\fi0 \}\
\fs14\
\f1 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Here\'27s the corresponding method for setting values:\
\fs14\
\pard \s20 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2413 \tx2889 \tx3421 \tx3953 \tx4457 \tx4933 \tx7005 \tx7509 \tx8013 \tx8517 \tx9021 \tx9525 \tx10029 \tx10533 \tx11037 \tx11513 \f2 \fs24 - (void)tableView:(NSTableView\'80*)aTableView\
\fi0     setObjectValue:anObject\
\fi0     forTableColumn:(NSTableColumn\'80*)aTableColumn\
\fi0     row:(int)rowIndex\
\fi0 \{\
\fi0     id theRecord;\
\fi0 \
    NSParameterAssert(rowIndex >= 0 && rowIndex < [records count]);\
\fi0     theRecord = [records objectAtIndex:rowIndex];\
\fi0     [theRecord setObject:anObject forKey:[aTableColumn identifier]];\
\fi0     return;\
\fi0 \}\
\fs14\
\f1 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Finally, \b numberOfRowsInTableView:\b0  simply returns the count of the NSArray:\
\fs14\
\pard \s20 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2413 \tx2889 \tx3421 \tx3953 \tx4457 \tx4933 \tx7005 \tx7509 \tx8013 \tx8517 \tx9021 \tx9525 \tx10029 \tx10533 \tx11037 \tx11513 \f2 \fs24 - (int)numberOfRowsInTableView:(NSTableView *)aTableView\
\fi0 \{\
\fi0     return [records count];\
\fi0 \}\
\fs14\
\f1 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 In each case, the NSTableView that sends the message is provided as \i aTableView\i0 . A data source object that manages several sets of data can choose the appropriate set based on which NSTableView sends the message.\
\fs14\
